    // page range, both of which were wrong before.
    const REFS = [
      "Hernán MA, Robins JM. Using big data to emulate a target trial when a randomized trial is not available. Am J Epidemiol 2016;183(8):758-764. doi:10.1093/aje/kwv254",
      "Hernán MA. How to estimate the effect of treatment duration on survival outcomes using observational data. BMJ 2018;360:k182. doi:10.1136/bmj.k182",
      "Maringe C, Benitez Majano S, Exarchakou A, et al. Reflection on modern methods: trial emulation in the presence of immortal-time bias. Assessing the benefit of major surgery for elderly lung cancer patients using observational data. Int J Epidemiol 2020;49(5):1719-1729. doi:10.1093/ije/dyaa057",
      "Gaber CE, Hanson KA, Kim S, Lund JL, Lee TA, Murray EJ. The clone-censor-weight method in pharmacoepidemiologic research: foundations and methodological implementation. Curr Epidemiol Rep 2024;11(3):164-174. doi:10.1007/s40471-024-00346-2",
      "Young JG, Stensrud MJ, Tchetgen Tchetgen EJ, Hernán MA. A causal framework for classical statistical estimands in failure-time settings with competing events. Stat Med 2020;39(8):1199-1236. doi:10.1002/sim.8471",
      "Gran JM, Røysland K, Wolbers M, et al. A sequential Cox approach for estimating the causal effect of treatment in the presence of time-dependent confounding applied to data from the Swiss HIV Cohort Study. Stat Med 2010;29(26):2757-2768. doi:10.1002/sim.4048",
      "Cashin AG, Hansford HJ, Hernán MA, et al. Transparent reporting of observational studies emulating a target trial — the TARGET statement. JAMA 2025;334(12):1084-1093. doi:10.1001/jama.2025.13350",
    ];

    const buildMarkdown = (s) => {
      const today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);
      const A = or(s.stratA, "Strategy A — initiate treatment within the grace period");
      const B = or(s.stratB, "Strategy B — do not initiate during the grace period");
      return `# ${or(s.title, "(Untitled clone-censor-weight study)")}

_Study protocol · clone-censor-weight target-trial emulation · drafted ${today}_

${window.PC.abstractMd(pcOf(s))}
## 1. Background and rationale
This study emulates a target trial to estimate the **per-protocol effect** of two treatment strategies that cannot be distinguished at time zero and are only defined by what happens over a **grace period** of ${daysTxt(s.grace)}. A conventional "as-treated at baseline" analysis would misclassify person-time and induce **immortal-time bias**, because treatment received at time *t* can only be observed in patients who survived to *t*; a "new-user" comparison cannot represent a *deferred / sustained* strategy at all. The clone-censor-weight (CCW) approach removes these biases by explicitly emulating the trial's assignment, adherence, and censoring structure (Hernán 2016; Hernán 2018; Maringe 2020).

## 2. Causal question (target trial)
Among **${or(s.population, "(population)")}**, what is the effect of **${A}** versus **${B}** on **${or(s.outcome, "(outcome)")}**?

## 3. Study design
A **clone-censor-weight** emulation of the target trial, using observational data from **${or(s.data, "(data source)")}**.

## 4. Time zero
${stop(or(s.timezero, "(define time zero — a point at which every patient is compatible with, and eligible for, all strategies)"))} Eligibility, strategy assignment, and the start of follow-up are all aligned at time zero.

## 5. Treatment strategies
- **${A}**
- **${B}**
- **Grace period:** ${daysTxt(s.grace)} from time zero, during which the data are still compatible with either strategy.

## 6. Cloning
At time zero each eligible patient is **cloned** once into every strategy arm. Each clone is assigned to, and followed under, one strategy. Because assignment is made by construction rather than by anything about the patient, the baseline covariate distributions are identical across arms and the assigned strategy is unconfounded at time zero.

Until a patient acts, their data are compatible with *both* strategies, so their person-time and any event they experience inside the grace period contribute to **both** arms; this is what removes immortal-time bias (Maringe 2020). It also means the arms are not independent samples, and the number of records exceeds the number of patients — with two consequences for Sections 10 and 11.

## 7. Artificial censoring (deviation)
${or(s.censor, "A clone is censored when its observed data first become incompatible with its assigned strategy (e.g. failure to initiate by the end of the grace period, or initiation in the deferred arm). Administrative censoring applies at disenrolment or end of study.")}

The two arms deviate on different schedules, and this shapes the weight models in Section 8: a clone in the **initiate** arm can only deviate at one time — the end of the grace period, when it becomes clear that treatment was not started — whereas a clone in the **deferred** arm can deviate at any moment inside the grace period. Maringe et al. therefore fit a time-varying (survival) model in the deferred arm and a time-fixed model in the initiate arm.

## 8. Inverse-probability-of-censoring weights (IPCW)
Artificial censoring is informative: the clones that remain uncensored in an arm are exactly those whose treatment behaviour matched it, and that behaviour is associated with prognosis. Each clone therefore receives a **stabilised inverse-probability-of-censoring weight** — the inverse of its estimated probability of remaining uncensored — so that the clones still in the risk set stand in for those removed and the arms stay comparable through the grace period (Maringe 2020). Censoring is modelled over follow-up time using baseline covariates measured over their look-back windows (**${rowsText(s.covlist)}**), with inclusion criteria assessed over ${daysTxt(s.incllookback)} and exclusions (${rowsText(s.excllist)}), together with time-varying predictors:

${(s.ipcw || "(list the baseline and time-varying predictors of deviation — e.g. age, sex, comorbidity and bleeding-risk scores, disease severity, concomitant therapy, and post-baseline events)").split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean).map((l) => "- " + l).join("\n") || "- (covariates)"}

**Separate weight models are fitted for the treatment and the control arm** (Section 7). The events treated as informative censoring and weighted by IPCW (each mapping to the assigned strategy) are: ${mergeList(s.ipcwReasonsPick, s.ipcwReasonsCustom).join("; ") || "(none selected — the artificial censoring created by cloning would then be left unadjusted)"}.

Weights are cumulative over follow-up. We report the distribution of the weights (mean, range, and extreme percentiles) and the weighted standardised mean differences between arms, and we confirm that the weights remove the imbalance that artificial censoring creates before the primary analysis is run. ${mergeList(s.sensitivityPick, s.sensitivityCustom).some((x) => /truncat/i.test(x)) ? "Truncation at the 1st/99th percentiles is examined as a pre-specified sensitivity analysis (Section 13); the primary analysis uses untruncated weights." : "Truncation is not applied in the primary analysis; if the weight distribution proves extreme, a truncated-weight analysis will be added as a documented amendment."}

**Identifying assumptions** (Maringe 2020): (i) every predictor of deviation from the assigned strategy is measured and included; (ii) the weight models are correctly specified; (iii) positivity — the probability of deviating is non-zero at every time within the grace period, for every patient; and (iv) consistency. Assumption (i) is not testable; assumption (iii) fails silently if the grace period is long enough that some covariate pattern always (or never) initiates, so we inspect the weight distribution for extreme values as a diagnostic.

## 9. Follow-up and outcome
${stop(or(s.followup, "Follow-up runs from time zero until the outcome, death, disenrolment, or the end of the study period, whichever comes first."))} Maximum follow-up per patient: **${daysTxt(s.fudays)}** from time zero. Outcome: **${or(s.outcome, "(outcome)")}**.

## 10. Estimand and effect measure
The primary estimand is the **per-protocol effect** — what would have happened had every patient received and adhered to the assigned strategy. For comparison we also report an **as-started (intention-to-treat-analogue) effect** from a conventional analysis (Section 11). We report ${effectText(s)}, with 95% confidence intervals from a **non-parametric bootstrap**.
${usesHR(s.effect) ? "\n> **Caveat on the hazard ratio.** During the grace period the two arms contain the same people, so their hazards are equal by construction and the proportional-hazards assumption is violated by design. Maringe et al. (2020) advise against analysing cloned data with a simple Cox model, and against reading the hazard ratio causally, because it averages conditional time-specific effects over a risk set whose composition changes. The absolute contrast (difference in survival at the end of follow-up, or in restricted mean survival time) is the interpretable measure here.\n" : ""}
## 11. Statistical analysis
**Primary — clone-censor-weight (per-protocol):**
1. Clone the eligible cohort into the strategy arms and apply the censoring rule.
2. Fit the arm-specific censoring model(s) and compute the stabilised IPCW.
3. Estimate the weighted survival / cumulative-incidence curve in each arm (weighted Kaplan–Meier, or a **weighted pooled logistic regression** for the discrete-time hazard with the assigned strategy, follow-up time, its quadratic term, and a strategy × time interaction).
4. Contrast the curves for the effect measure(s)${usesRMST(s.effect) ? ", taking the RMST in each arm as the area under its weighted survival curve from time zero to " + horizon(s).replace(/^the end of follow-up/, "the horizon") + " and reporting the difference" : ""}.
5. Obtain 95% CIs by **non-parametric bootstrap**: resample **patients** (not clones) with replacement, then repeat steps 1–4 — cloning, censoring, weight estimation and outcome modelling — inside every replicate. Model-based standard errors must not be used: cloning inflates the apparent number of events, so they are too small (Maringe 2020).

**Comparison — conventional analysis (as-started, ITT-analogue):** start follow-up at the index date, balance the arms by **propensity-score matching** (standardised mean difference < 0.1 as the balance criterion), and handle the competing risk of death with **Fine–Gray sub-distribution** and **cause-specific Cox** models. The gap between this and the CCW estimate indexes the time-lag / immortal-time bias removed.${mergeList(s.ipcwReasonsPick, s.ipcwReasonsCustom).some((x) => /^death/i.test(x)) ? "\n\n**Note on death.** Death is listed above as an event censored and weighted by IPCW. Censoring at death and weighting for it estimates a *hypothetical* effect — the effect that would be seen if death could not occur — which is a different estimand from the total effect in a world where death competes with the outcome. State which of the two is intended, and report the competing-risk analysis alongside it (Young 2020)." : ""}

## 12. Subgroup analyses
${(mergeList(s.subgroupsPick, s.subgroupsCustom).length ? mergeList(s.subgroupsPick, s.subgroupsCustom) : ["(none pre-specified)"]).map((l) => "- " + l).join("\n")}

## 13. Sensitivity analyses
${(mergeList(s.sensitivityPick, s.sensitivityCustom).length ? mergeList(s.sensitivityPick, s.sensitivityCustom) : ["(none pre-specified)"]).map((l) => "- " + l).join("\n")}

${window.PC.tailMd(pcOf(s))}

## References
${REFS.map((r) => "- " + r).join("\n")}
`;
    };

    const computeChecks = (s) => {
      const checks = [];
      const grace = parseInt(s.grace) || 0, fu = parseInt(s.fudays) || 0, covRows = parseRows(s.covlist), lb = covRows.length;
      const reasons = mergeList(s.ipcwReasonsPick, s.ipcwReasonsCustom);
      if (blank(s.grace)) checks.push({ level: "error", msg: "Grace period is empty. It is not assumed to be anything — the protocol will print “(not specified)” until you set it. With no grace window the two strategies cannot be distinguished by cloning." });
      else if (grace <= 0) checks.push({ level: "error", msg: "Grace period is 0 — with no grace window the two strategies cannot be distinguished by cloning, and every clone deviates at time zero. Set a grace period > 0." });
      if (blank(s.fudays)) checks.push({ level: "error", msg: "Maximum follow-up is empty. The diagram shows a placeholder bar labelled “length not set”, and the protocol prints “(not specified)” — nothing is assumed." });
      else if (fu > 0 && grace >= fu) checks.push({ level: "error", msg: "The grace period (" + grace + " d) is ≥ the maximum follow-up (" + fu + " d), so the arms are identical for the whole of follow-up and there is no post-grace period in which they can differ. The grace band in the diagram now overruns the follow-up bar to show this. Shorten the grace period or lengthen follow-up." });
      if (lb <= 0) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "No covariate look-back — baseline confounders and eligibility are assessed only at time zero. The IPCW models need these covariates, so without them the weights cannot adjust the artificial censoring." });
      const zeroRows = [...covRows, ...parseRows(s.excllist)].filter((r) => !r.days).map((r) => r.name);
      if (zeroRows.length) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "These rows have a look-back of 0 days, which means the criterion is assessed only on the index day itself: " + zeroRows.join("; ") + ". A row is read as “Name | days” — check for a missing or non-numeric value after the “|”." });
      if (!s.timezero || !s.timezero.trim()) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "Define time zero. It must be a point at which every patient is still eligible for, and compatible with, every strategy — otherwise cloning assigns people to strategies they could never have followed." });
      if (!s.censor || !s.censor.trim()) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "No deviation / censoring rule — clones must be censored when their data stop matching the assigned strategy. State the rule for each arm separately; they are not symmetric." });
      if (!s.ipcw || !s.ipcw.trim()) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "No IPCW covariates listed. Cloning removes confounding of the assigned strategy, but the censoring it introduces is informative; without the weights the CCW estimate is not less biased than the naive one, only differently biased." });
      if (!reasons.length) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "No censoring events selected for IPCW — pick the events (discontinuation, switch, add-on…) that create informative censoring under each strategy." });
      if (usesRMST(s.effect) && blank(s.fudays)) checks.push({ level: "error", msg: "RMST is selected but no maximum follow-up is set. Restricted mean survival time is the area under the survival curve up to a horizon τ, so it has no value until τ is fixed — and because τ must be pre-specified rather than chosen after seeing the curves, set it here." });
      if (usesHR(s.effect)) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "You have chosen the hazard ratio. In a cloned design the arms contain the same people during the grace period, so their hazards are equal by construction and proportional hazards cannot hold. Maringe et al. (2020) recommend against a simple Cox model here and against a causal reading of the HR. Prefer the difference in survival at the end of follow-up, or in restricted mean survival time." });
      if (reasons.some((x) => /^death/i.test(x))) checks.push({ level: "warn", msg: "Death is marked as a censoring event weighted by IPCW. That targets a hypothetical estimand — the effect if death could not occur — not the total effect in a world where death competes with the outcome (Young 2020). If death is a competing event rather than a loss of information, say so and analyse it as one. If the outcome is death, it should not appear here at all." });
      checks.push({ level: "info", msg: "CCW targets the per-protocol effect: what would have happened had everyone received and adhered to the assigned strategy. It is not the intention-to-treat effect, and it is not what a conventional as-started analysis estimates." });
      checks.push({ level: "info", msg: "Confidence intervals come from a non-parametric bootstrap that resamples patients and re-runs cloning, censoring and weighting in each replicate. Model-based standard errors are too small here, because cloning inflates the apparent number of events (Maringe 2020)." });
      if (!s.population || !s.population.trim()) checks.push({ level: "info", msg: "Specify the population." });
      if (!s.outcome || !s.outcome.trim()) checks.push({ level: "info", msg: "Specify the outcome." });
      if (!s.stratA || !s.stratB) checks.push({ level: "info", msg: "Specify both treatment strategies (A and B)." });
      return checks;
    };

    // No bare `catch {}` around rendering: a swallowed error here is how a
    // diagram once stayed blank for weeks. Failures are logged, and each stage is
    // isolated so one broken panel cannot take the others down with it.
    const render = () => {
      const s = readForm();
      preview.textContent = buildMarkdown(s);
      try { renderScheme(s); } catch (e) { console.error("[ccw] diagram failed", e); }
      try { window.renderChecks(document.getElementById("checks"), computeChecks(s)); } catch (e) { console.error("[ccw] checks failed", e); }
      try { localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(s)); } catch (e) { console.warn("[ccw] autosave failed", e); }
    };

    (() => {
      try {
        // A ?seed= link wins over the saved draft; PC.restore falls back to
        // localStorage on its own and tells the user which one it used.
        const seeded = (window.PC && PC.restore)
          ? PC.restore(form, STORAGE_KEY, (s) => PC.applySeed(form, s)) === "seed"
          : false;
